MiBench kernels as HLSFactory designs (Vitis HLS flows)
=======================================================

Source: MiBench, "A free, commercially representative embedded benchmark
suite", M. R. Guthaus, J. S. Ringenberg, D. Ernst, T. M. Austin, T. Mudge,
R. B. Brown, WWC-4, 2001.  http://vhosts.eecs.umich.edu/mibench/
The original, unmodified sources are the *.tar.gz archives in this directory
(one per MiBench category).  Each design below keeps the copyright and
licence notices of the code it was derived from; see the file headers.

Each subdirectory is one design:

  hlsfactory.toml        design config (VitisHLSSynthFlow + VitisHLSCsimFlow)
  hls.tcl                Vitis HLS script (phase argument: csim | syn)
  dataset_hls.tcl        synthesis entry point used by VitisHLSSynthFlow
  dataset_hls_csim.tcl   C simulation entry point used by VitisHLSCsimFlow
  *.c / *.h              the kernel (top function named in hls.tcl)
  tb.c                   self-checking testbench, prints PASS and returns 0

Kernels are extracted from the MiBench programs, not the whole programs:
file I/O, malloc, recursion, function pointers and printing are removed and
the data is processed in fixed-size buffers so the top function is
synthesizable.  Algorithms and constants are those of the original code.

  design            MiBench source                 top function
  ----------------  -----------------------------  ------------------
  bitcount          automotive/bitcount            bitcnts
  qsort             automotive/qsort (small)       qsort_words
  basicmath_cubic   automotive/basicmath cubic.c   solve_cubics
  basicmath_isqrt   automotive/basicmath isqrt.c   usqrt_array
  susan_smoothing   automotive/susan -s            susan_smoothing
  susan_principle   automotive/susan -e -p         susan_principle
  susan_edges       automotive/susan -e            susan_edges
  susan_corners     automotive/susan -c            susan_corners
  dijkstra          network/dijkstra (small)       dijkstra_queries
  stringsearch      office/stringsearch (small)    stringsearch
  stringsearch_bmh  office/stringsearch bmh*srch.c  stringsearch_bmh
  patricia          network/patricia               patricia_trace
  sha               security/sha                   sha_digest
  blowfish          security/blowfish (bf e/d)     blowfish_cfb64
  rijndael          security/rijndael              rijndael_ecb
  crc32             telecomm/CRC32                 crc32buf
  fft               telecomm/FFT                   fft_float
  adpcm_encode      telecomm/adpcm rawcaudio       adpcm_coder
  adpcm_decode      telecomm/adpcm rawdaudio       adpcm_decoder
  gsm_encode        telecomm/gsm toast             gsm_encode_block
  gsm_decode        telecomm/gsm untoast           gsm_decode_block

Notable adaptations
  * rijndael: the original is Gladman's variable block/key size AES with
    run-time table generation; the design is a compact AES-128 (FIPS-197)
    ECB implementation with constant tables.
  * bitcount: the recursive ntbl_bitcnt() is iterative; the function pointer
    table is unrolled into direct calls.
  * qsort: the C library qsort() is an iterative quicksort over fixed-length
    words, same (descending strcmp) order as the benchmark comparator.
  * dijkstra: the malloc'ed linked-list queue is a circular buffer.
  * basicmath_cubic: 'long double' temporaries are 'double'.
  * patricia: nodes live in a static pool addressed by index and insertR() is
    a loop.  Only pat_search()/pat_insert() are ported (the driver does not
    call pat_remove()) and a node holds one mask, since every entry the
    driver inserts is a /32 host address.
  * stringsearch_bmh: the three Boyer-Moore-Horspool searchers (exact,
    case-insensitive, accent-folding) that the MiBench Makefile builds next
    to the Pratt-Boyer-Moore one used by the driver, run on the same pairs.
  * susan_edges / susan_corners: the full "-e" (response, direction,
    non-maximum suppression, thinning, drawing) and "-c" (response, corner
    test, 7x7 suppression, drawing) pipelines.  The unrolled 37-pixel mask
    walks are loops; the corner list has a fixed capacity.
  * gsm_*: the original GSM 06.10 library files (add, long_term, rpe,
    short_term, table, plus code/lpc/preprocess/gsm_encode or
    decode/gsm_decode), with longword/ulongword made exactly 32 bit and the
    MiBench build options (SASR, STUPID_COMPILER) set in private.h.  The
    malloc'ed codec handle is a local state, and a wrapper codes 16 frames.
  * sha: original SHA by default, NIST's modified SHA with -DUSE_MODIFIED_SHA,
    as in the MiBench Makefile.

Test oracles: gsm_* compare against the reference data shipped with MiBench
(data/small.au.run.gsm for the encoder; the original library's output for
the decoder); adpcm and susan_* compare against hashes produced by running
the original MiBench code on the same input; blowfish and rijndael against
OpenSSL and the standard test vectors; the others against independent
reference implementations in tb.c.

Not included (not kernel-shaped, depend on the OS, files or large libraries):
consumer/* (jpeg, lame, mad, tiff*, typeset), office/{ghostscript,ispell,
rsynth,sphinx}, security/pgp, and all of the *_large variants.
